<!-- PROSE + ASIDE -->
<div class="prose-body">
  <div class="prose-col">

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where this started</h2>
      <p>
        I spent three years working inside a digital agency. Good work, good clients, genuinely talented team.
        But the operations were a disaster. Every Monday standup was 45 minutes of catching up on things
        that should have been visible all week. Every client onboarding was a different improvised process.
        Every revision request lived in a different Slack thread.
      </p>
      <p>
        I started building Notion systems to fix the specific problems I was watching happen around me.
        First a client portal that replaced our onboarding process. Then a project tracker that gave
        our creative director visibility across all active work. Then a feedback log that got revision
        requests out of email and into something we could actually manage.
      </p>

      <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>The work was never the problem. The systems around the work were the problem.</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        After leaving the agency I started building these systems for other agencies as a consultant.
        The same problems kept appearing in every client — different industries, different sizes,
        same chaos. So I packaged the solutions as templates that any agency could set up themselves
        in an afternoon rather than paying for a custom build.
      </p>

      <h2>How I got here</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-dot"><div class="tl-dot-inner dot-gray"></div></div>
        <div class="tl-content">
          <div class="tl-year">2020</div>
          <div class="tl-title">Joined a digital agency as operations lead</div>
          <div class="tl-desc">Responsible for making 40 simultaneous client projects not collapse into chaos. They mostly did anyway.</div>
        </div>
