          <div class="tl-desc">The same five problems kept appearing. Started packaging the solutions into repeatable systems.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-dot"><div class="tl-dot-inner dot-purple"></div></div>
        <div class="tl-content">
          <div class="tl-year">2025</div>
          <div class="tl-title">Launched LayerBase</div>
          <div class="tl-desc">Packaged the consulting systems as self-serve templates. First sale within 48 hours of going live.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What I actually believe</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="beliefs">
      <div class="belief">
        <div class="belief-num">01</div>
        <div class="belief-text">
          <strong>Operations are a competitive advantage</strong>
          Agencies with better systems deliver better work, retain better clients, and scale without burning their team out. It's not an admin concern — it's a strategic one.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="belief">
        <div class="belief-num">02</div>
        <div class="belief-text">
          <strong>Simple beats sophisticated every time</strong>
          A system your team actually uses beats a perfect system nobody opens. Every template I build is designed around the minimum structure that solves the problem — not the maximum features I could add.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="belief">
        <div class="belief-num">03</div>
        <div class="belief-text">
          <strong>Clients notice when you're organised</strong>
          A client who logs into a professional portal on day one forms a different impression than one who gets a welcome email with five attachments. The system is part of your product.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="belief">
        <div class="belief-num">04</div>
        <div class="belief-text">
          <strong>The tool is rarely the problem</strong>
          Notion is good. So is ClickUp, Asana, and Linear. What breaks agencies is not the wrong tool — it's no consistent process for using the tool they have. I focus on the process first.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <h2>What I can help you with</h2>
      <p>
        If you run or work in a digital agency and your operations feel like they're held together
        with duct tape — <strong>start with the templates</strong>. The client portal in particular
        solves the most immediate pain: onboarding chaos and the "just checking in" email cycle.
        Most people have it running within an afternoon.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your situation is more complex — multiple teams, unusual workflows, specific integrations
        you need — I offer done-for-you Notion builds. We start with a free 20-minute audit call
        where I look at your current setup and tell you exactly where the bottlenecks are.
        No sales pitch, just a diagnosis.
      </p>
      <p>
        And if you want to follow the thinking behind the systems — the blog covers case studies,
        operational breakdowns, and honest takes on what works and what doesn't in agency ops.
        One post per week, no filler.
      </p>
    </div>

